2026-09-03T10:15Z Minh Ha-Duong note THE PRICE IS MEASURED, THE BENEFIT IS NOT, AND THE BENEFIT SHRANK when the fork's real state was read. The two notes above price the pack route at roughly 2,2x the bytes and 9x the time of the flat extraction it would replace. What nothing here has measured is what those buy, and the author put the question directly: are packs needed at all, or does flat text suffice? The flat path reaches further than this ticket assumed, all of it already built: seg/1 cuts entries at 0,996 and 0,988 confidence on a FR thesis and an EN book; `extractPdfOutline` gives the document's own outline; `extractPdfPages` re-extracts REAL page ranges, though R24 is NOT thereby met today -- see the correction entry below; and `pdf-locate.ts` already recovers a rectangle on the page by matching passage text against pdf.js text runs. The locator was this ticket's stated reason to want packs, and it is largely already served. WHAT REMAINS UNIQUE TO A PACK, narrower than the body above claims: a rect reliable by construction rather than by a text match that refuses on a doubtful case; block typing (math, list, image) that flat text has none of; and text from a different extractor, free of the running-header and glued-paragraph artefacts -- two of seg/1's three fixes of 2026-09-02 are workarounds for exactly those, which is evidence FOR the pack that this ticket had not counted. AGAINST: two packs are a probe and not a benchmark (heading recall 7 of 7 on one document and 4 on the other, and they disagree, SYNC.md), and packs hold less text than the flat cache. CONSEQUENCE: the exit criterion comparing our blocks to theirs is no longer a late gate but the FIRST question, and it needs a third arm -- the flat path as it actually stands, not as this ticket imagined it. A benefit that does not beat seg/1 plus pdf.js does not earn 2,2x the bytes and 24,6 h.

